{"attack":true,"case":"The target asserts the single dominant cross-city mechanism is geometry (building height / sky view factor), but its own countercondition concedes the claim fails if aridity or vegetation deficit correlates more strongly with nocturnal UHI — and aridity/vegetation deficit is the empirically dominant cross-city modulator, making the geometry-first claim a false identification of the single best explanation.\nThe problem asks for the single mechanism that best explains why the nocturnal UHI gap is larger in some cities than others. The target answers that the cross-city modulator 'is geometry' and that intensity 'scales most strongly with built density and building height.' But the target's own counterconditions admit defeat if 'the cross-city gap correlates more strongly with aridity or vegetation deficit than with building height.' In the literature the cross-city nighttime UHI intensity is primarily governed by the urban–rural moisture/evapotranspiration contrast: drier, less vegetated surroundings lose latent cooling and warm less at night, while irrigated/green rural areas remain cooler, producing a larger gap. That is exactly the aridity/vegetation pathway the target lists as a subordinate factor but then demotes, contradicting the 'single best' requirement.\ncites: d5522d0ffff30a289f4f772c1b39279531ad9c1d712fdaa85f3dd9bb27263941","premise":"The target's stated single dominant mechanism for cross-city variation is geometry; its own countercondition exposes that as wrong if aridity/vegetation deficit dominates, which it does.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"d5522d0ffff30a289f4f772c1b39279531ad9c1d712fdaa85f3dd9bb27263941","quote":"The cross-city modulator is geometry: cities with deeper canyons and denser buildings retain more stored heat and cool more slowly, producing a larger nocturnal gap."},{"block":"d5522d0ffff30a289f4f772c1b39279531ad9c1d712fdaa85f3dd9bb27263941","quote":"If the cross-city gap correlates more strongly with aridity or vegetation deficit than with building height, the claimed modulator is wrong."}]}